    updated: published,
    category: 'Guides',
    tags: ['Word Guess rules', 'Five letter word game', 'Word puzzle instructions', 'Word Guess online'],
    excerpt: 'Understand what each tile color means and how the six-guess limit shapes a careful word-solving routine.',
    images: blogImages('word'),
    body: [
      'Word Guess rules are straightforward: find the hidden five-letter word in six tries. Each guess must be a real English word, and the colored tiles tell you what you learned. Green means a letter is correct and in the right position. Yellow means the letter belongs in the word but sits somewhere else. Gray means that letter is not part of the answer, subject to repeated-letter clues. Read the colors as evidence, then [play Word Guess](/games/word) to try a round.',
      '@image:0',
      '## What a guess does',
      '### Use each row to test a question',
      'A guess is a complete five-letter word. After you submit it, the game checks each letter against the hidden answer and colors the tiles. You have six rows, so every word should give you useful information. An opening guess tests several letters at once, while a later guess can place a known letter or test a possible position.',
      'Use the keyboard or on-screen letter controls, then submit the full word. A guess must be accepted by the game’s word list. If the word is not recognized, it does not count as a valid attempt. This gives you a chance to correct a typo or choose an accepted word before you spend one of the six rows.',
      '## Read green, yellow and gray',
      'A green tile confirms two facts: the letter belongs in the answer and it is already in the right position. Keep it fixed in every later guess. A yellow tile confirms the letter is present but not in that position. Use it again somewhere else, taking care not to move it back into a spot already marked yellow for that letter.',
      'Gray feedback usually rules out a letter, but repeated letters can make the result less obvious. If a guess contains the same letter more than once, the answer may include only one copy. A second copy can receive gray even when the letter itself belongs in the word. Count the colors together and avoid concluding that a gray tile always means every copy of that letter is absent.',
      '@image:1',
      '## Build a useful first guess',
      'Choose a common five-letter word that tests several distinct letters. The first guess is for information, not for proving that the answer is a familiar word. If it receives several gray tiles, you can remove those letters from future options. If it reveals a green or yellow, carry that constraint forward. Avoid using a very unusual word unless you have a reason to test a particular letter pattern.',
      'There is no single opening that guarantees a win because the hidden word changes. A practical first word uses letters that appear in many common words and avoids repeating the same letter unless you deliberately want to test duplication. Once you see the feedback, make the next guess reflect it rather than submitting a second unrelated word.',
      '## Turn clues into a candidate pattern',
      'After each row, write down the constraints mentally or on paper: fixed green positions, yellow letters that need new positions, and letters the clues rule out. For example, a pattern like _ A _ _ E is only a starting shape. The yellow letters add more restrictions, and gray letters remove candidates. A possible answer must satisfy every row, not just the latest one.',
      'When you have several candidate words, choose a guess that tests a remaining uncertainty. You may need to determine whether a letter is present, whether a known letter can fit in a particular position or whether the answer repeats a letter. A test word can be useful even when you do not think it is the answer, as long as it separates plausible options and fits the rules.',
      '## Hard mode changes how you guess',
      'Hard mode requires you to use every green and yellow clue in your next guess. This prevents you from spending a row on a completely unrelated test word. It can make the puzzle more demanding because your guesses must honor existing constraints while still learning something new. The setting is optional and can be turned on from the menu.',
      'In hard mode, keep each known green in place and include every yellow letter. Before submitting, check that a yellow letter has moved to a different position. If you have several yellow letters, ensure they all appear in the word and do not collide with fixed greens. The constraint makes careless guesses easier to spot before you spend an attempt.',
      '## Use hints and stars carefully',
      'A hint reveals one letter in its correct place, but the level’s score is capped at two stars after using a hint. That makes a hint useful when you want to keep progressing, but less suitable if you are aiming for the highest rating. Decide what matters for the round: solve the word with assistance or continue trying to preserve the star score.',
      'The game awards three stars for solving in three guesses or fewer. That target rewards efficient openings and careful clue use, but it should not encourage random words. If a hint or an extra guess helps you learn the letter feedback, treat it as practice. A steady reasoning process is more repeatable than chasing a score with a guess that ignores your earlier rows.',
      '## A clean solving routine',
      [
        'Submit a valid five-letter word with varied letters.',
        'Keep greens fixed and move yellow letters to new positions.',
        'Remove gray letters unless repeated-letter clues show a duplicate case.',
        'Choose later guesses to test a specific uncertainty.',
        'Use a hint only after deciding whether its star cap is acceptable.',
      ],
      'When the clues appear inconsistent, revisit each row. Check whether you reused a yellow letter in the same position, moved a green or misread a gray tile caused by a duplicate. The game has not changed the rules between turns; one missed constraint can make every candidate look wrong. Correcting the notes often reveals the next word.',
      '## Start a word round',
      'Play [Word Guess free online](/play/word) and use the first rows to learn how the board marks feedback. If you want a similar language challenge without a six-try word answer, browse [free online puzzle games](/blogs/best-free-online-puzzle-games). For a non-word pattern game, [Memory Sequence](/games/memory) asks you to repeat a signal order instead of deducing letters.',
    ],
    faq: [
      { q: 'How many guesses do you get in Word Guess?', a: 'You have six attempts to find the hidden five-letter word.' },
      { q: 'What does a yellow tile mean?', a: 'The letter is in the hidden word, but it is not in the position where you guessed it.' },
      { q: 'What does a gray tile mean?', a: 'The letter is usually absent. If your guess repeats a letter, count all the feedback before ruling out every copy.' },
      { q: 'How does hard mode work?', a: 'Hard mode requires you to use every green and yellow clue in your next guess.' },
    ],
